data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1794575850323,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched Apollo from Kennedy Space Center on July 16 at 13:32 UTC; only the command module returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water to the command module.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin descended aboard the LM Eagle and landed in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC, while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material and speaking with President Nixon.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth on July 24, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the national goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the Apollo 1 fire in January 1967 was a severe setback.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong declared, 'That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind,' upon stepping onto the lunar surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples returned by the mission led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Saturn V\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"United States\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed mission to the Moon was the challenge intended to serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s ended.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be among the most impressive and important for long-range space exploration, and among the most difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding other engines and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Urgent National Needs\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's words suggest that going to the Moon would be a national effort, since everyone must work to put a man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that space conquest deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered the question of why choose the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the US chooses to reach the Moon this decade because the goal organizes and measures the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener called it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Khrushchev, who declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint lunar expedition to the UN General Assembly in September 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Premier of the Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"A space rendezvous is an orbital maneuver in which two spacecraft navigate through space and meet up.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo had three parts: a command module that returned to Earth, a service module providing propulsion, power, oxygen and water, and a lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar orbit rendezvous design meant a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, could launch the spacecraft.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"Project Gemini developed technologies and techniques required for Apollo; Apollo also used MOSFETs in the IMP and silicon IC chips in the AGC.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Saturn V\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo abruptly.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo program\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Urgent National Needs\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Atlantic\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Premier of the Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"ac435442795d0d5d5236e12b8d29fbe8885898a1cf5c106c06651847e7f7c7c8"}}

