, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit."},{"id":"4.5","text":"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital."}]},{"chunk":5,"points":[{"id":"5.1","text":"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position."},{"id":"5.2","text":"A crewed mission to the Moon was the challenge intended to serve this purpose."},{"id":"5.3","text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s ended."},{"id":"5.4","text":"Kennedy said the project would be among the most impressive and important for long-range space exploration, and among the most difficult and expensive."},{"id":"5.5","text":"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding other engines and unmanned exploration."}]},{"chunk":6,"points":[{"id":"6.1","text":"Kennedy's words suggest that going to the Moon would be a national effort, since everyone must work to put a man there."},{"id":"6.2","text":"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas."},{"id":"6.3","text":"Kennedy said outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all."},{"id":"6.4","text":"Kennedy argued that space conquest deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never come again."},{"id":"6.5","text":"Kennedy answered the question of why choose the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'"}]},{"chunk":7,"points":[{"id":"7.1","text":"Kennedy said the US chooses to reach the Moon this decade because the goal organizes and measures the best energies and skills."},{"id"